ric, exasperating and absorbing as ever.</p><p>By â€œthe moviesâ€, incidentally, he means Hollywood, with a side order of established European and east Asian arthouse: he takes us from the cine-prehistory of photographer <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2010/sep/04/eadweard-muybridge-exhibition-rebecca-solnit">Eadweard Muybridge</a> to Fritz Langâ€™s Metropolis, DW Griffithâ€™s Birth of a Nation with its sinister Ku Klux Klan hero-worship, Chaplin, Stroheim, Hitchcock, Capra, Renoir, Bogart, Marilyn Monroe in The Seven-Year Itch, Monroe Stahr in The Last Tycoon, Scorseseâ€™s GoodFellas, David Lynchâ€™s Mulholland Drive (â€œIs it drive or dream?â€), Kirk&nbsp;Douglas and his alleged rape of Natalie Wood, Barbara Lodenâ€™s Wanda, Sean Bakerâ€™s Anora, Tracey Eminâ€™s Why I Never Became a Dancer and much more. He subtitles his book a â€œrevisionistâ€ history.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/a-sudden-flicker-of-light-by-david-thomson-review-a-brilliantly-eccentric-history-of-the-movies">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9-0U-jMmSSC-H3Ju3VaDnRdcE5YuKMTL2IJxYolOUQsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;t2OMcc3TRfksKz_LRXqI3s-nWjn7N1Kp_p0ZO0do-scƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;b4ZacbAE1VeXSKUfrCojj579V25mMDxrC_-8Uh2nTDAƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Kj1rCgrxBftro2-TKTRICTppHllGP24zQgMxQUn-obgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CIjFQ-32xZ-3csujiQAux_Au7UmhbsL2v2HfuBx90owƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;dFDIM3R8nunj0nFiK7bG7qkgXYM3Q3mQIF0Tg6HjZPsƒdpairƒdleft